  {
   "oldText": "1. At instance start, check `programs/<task id>.py`. If present, run `transform(test_grid)` and submit its returned grid before requesting demos or changing the program.\n2. If the program is absent or its answer is rejected, request only the demonstrations needed to infer the rule. Separate structural clues from exact cell placement: for expansions, compare dimensions, preserved regions, block histograms, then positions; matching histograms proves only a permutation. For sparse, interrupted outlines, check whether markers trace an enclosing frame before filling gaps; if so, fill only missing border cells. For diagonal stripe patterns, compare cells by `r+c` and `r-c` to identify the stripe direction, then read demonstrated outputs along that diagonal index to infer the cycle, phase, and any unequal run widths. A period that merely fits sparse clues does not determine unseen colors: request another demo rather than filling unknown residues by guess or nearest-neighbor. Implement or fix `transform(grid)` and run it against every exact pair received; require exact input-output matches before submitting. When transcribing grids for local testing, assert row count and every row width against the stated dimensions; fix transcription errors before trusting results.\n3. Run the verified transform on the test input and copy its returned grid exactly into the required submit action; do not manually adjust cells. Keep the required action JSON as the final line.\n4. After verdict, save the transform that reproduces all received pairs at `programs/<task id>.py`, replacing the old version, then finish only when the instance is over.",
   "newText": "1. At instance start, check `programs/<task id>.py`. If present, run `transform(test_grid)` and submit its returned grid before requesting demos or changing the program.\n2. If no program exists or its answer is rejected, request only the demonstrations needed to infer the rule. Use the relevant branch checks:\n   - **Expansions:** Compare dimensions, preserved regions, block histograms, then positions. Matching histograms proves only a permutation, not exact placement.\n   - **Sparse interrupted outlines:** Check whether markers trace an enclosing frame before filling gaps; if so, fill only missing border cells.\n   - **Diagonal stripes:** Compare cells by `r+c` and `r-c` to identify stripe direction, then read demonstrated outputs along that diagonal index to infer the cycle, phase, and any unequal run widths. A period that merely fits sparse clues does not determine unseen colors; request another demo instead of guessing unknown residues or using nearest-neighbor fill.\n\n   Implement or fix `transform(grid)` and test it against every exact pair received; require exact input-output matches before submission. When transcribing grids for local testing, assert row count and every row width against the stated dimensions, and fix transcription errors before trusting results.\n3. Run the verified transform on the test input and copy its returned grid exactly into the required submit action; do not manually adjust cells. Keep the required action JSON as the final line.\n4. After verdict, save the transform that reproduces all received pairs at `programs/<task id>.py`, replacing the old version, then finish only when the instance is over."
